          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The terminology
            %em (commutative) ring spectrum
            refers either to a (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monoid"}> commutative
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+category"} stable homotopy category
            regarded as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            via the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smash+product+of+spectra"} smash product of spectra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+for+spectra"} model structure for spectra
            equipped with a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+smash+product+of+spectra"} symmetric smash product of spectra
          %p
            In the first case a ring spectrum is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            equipped with a unit and product operation, which is associative, unital (and commutative) just up to unspecified
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H-space"} H-space
            structure. Accordingly, these might be called “H-ring spectra”, but it is traditional to just call them “ring spectra.” (An
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H-infinity+ring+spectrum"} H-infinity ring spectrum
            (
            %a{:href => "#BrunerMayMcLureSteinberger86"}> Bruner-May-McClure-Steinberger 86
            ) is such an H-ring spectrum equipped with some extra structure modeling
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+power"} extended power
            operations.)
          %p
            In the second case the structure is much richer; and in good cases, such as for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/highly+structured+spectra"} highly structured spectra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-%E2%88%9E+ring"} A-∞ ring
            structure (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+ring"} E-∞ ring
            structure).
          %p
            To distinguish the two situations further qualification is being used. Sometimes one says
            %strong homotopy ring spectrum
            to explicitly refer to the first case (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Schwede12"} Schwede 12, chapter II 4.1
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/highly+structured+ring+spectrum"} highly structured ring spectrum
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brave+new+algebra"} brave new algebra
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+algebra"} higher algebra
          %p
            Since the concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            is the refinement of the concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
            to
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+theory"} (∞,1)-category theory
            %strong ring spectrum
            is the corresponding generalization of the notion of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"}> commutative
            )
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
          %div
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+algebra"} higher algebra
              %tbody
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
